ing R1–R3). Sealing content that a sealed bundle of the\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:2244:// selection (spec pose-review-approval-follows-children R4).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:2285:// another (spec pose-review-approval-follows-children R4, D3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:2336:// session's otherwise, with the model from routing.lead_models (R2, R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:3908:// ends (spec pose-review-readiness-before-seal R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:4082:\t\t\t// (spec pose-review-declared-threat-model R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:4084:\t\t\t\tblockers = append(blockers, \"finding \"+finding.ID+\" names accepted limit \"+firstNonempty(finding.Limit, \"(none)\")+\", which this bundle did not seal\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:4589:// pose-closeout-judges-the-manifest R1, R2). Without a resolver no manifest can be\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle.go:4655:// is a difference (spec pose-roadmap-close-keeps-its-approvals R1-R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go:132:\tAcceptedLimits []ReviewAcceptedLimit `json:\"accepted_limits,omitempty\"`\npose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go:73:\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+quoteLedgerText(trimmed)+\" is not written as '- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.'\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go:77:\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+m[1]+\" is declared twice\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go:82:\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, \"accepted limit \"+m[1]+\" does not say why it is accepted ('Accepted because ...')\")\nPOSE.md:1:# POSE — Project Operating Standard for Engineering\nPOSE.md:3:## 1) What it is\nPOSE.md:22:## 2) Principles\nPOSE.md:32:## 3) Structure\nPOSE.md:58:## 4) Key files\nPOSE.md:81:## 5) Flows per task type\nPOSE.md:96:### 5.1 Spec lifecycle\nPOSE.md:183:## 6) The `pose` CLI\nPOSE.md:188:# Scaffold and specs\nPOSE.md:196:pose spec-transfer preview --source <xref> --destination <xref> --map R1=equivalent:R1 [--json]\nPOSE.md:211:# `pose lint-spec <slug> --design-check` projects structured Assumption/Decision\nPOSE.md:212:# basis nodes read-only; it is advisory and never changes lifecycle state.\nPOSE.md:214:# Deterministic gates\nPOSE.md:224:# every gate above, plus `pose index` and `pose state`, also takes: [--json] [--json-out f] [--quiet] [--color auto|always|never]\nPOSE.md:234:# Governed closeout and review\nPOSE.md:250:# Delegated executions\nPOSE.md:263:# Project state and docs governance\nPOSE.md:271:# Discovery and metrics\nPOSE.md:286:# Assessment and extensions\nPOSE.md:290:# Artifact generation and maintenance\nPOSE.md:308:# Installation, MCP, contributions and telemetry\nPOSE.md:332:# Release lifecycle\nPOSE.md:340:### Command reference\nPOSE.md:346:- `spec-transfer` — explicit cross-project authority transfer. `preview` emits a deterministic digest-bound JSON plan without writing. Requirement mappings are explicit (`R1=equivalent:R1`, `R2=withdrawn`, `-=pending:R3`); apply requires the same digest plus one `--authorize-project` for every affected repository, and resume verifies the saved plan copies and receipts. Transfer is refused until both endpoints and every affected project adopt review-policy schema 4 (`qualified_artifact_refs_version: 1`, `spec_authority_transfer_version: 1`). Current dependency/membership links follow the verified redirect; the MCP tool `pose_spec_transfer_status` reads the journal only within its authorized project. `--mode reconcile-terminal` retires a coordinator spec onto an executor that is already `done` with a terminal closeout in its own project: the executor file is never written (apply only verifies its digest), the map is N:M over every source requirement and may target another spec with `destination_ref`, `withdrawn` needs a `rationale`, and `pending` needs a `rationale` plus an open target spec, recorded in the schema-2 redirect as `open_obligations`; `--map-file` reads the map as JSON. Engines without this mode refuse schema-2 plans and redirects. No automatic commits, pushes or policy adoption occur. Split-section specs without a single `spec.md` are reported as unsupported rather than rewritten.\nPOSE.md:389:- `action <open|list|show|resolve|cancel|waive|invalidate>` (specs `pose-action-requests`, `pose-action-request-resolution`) — material requests to a person or an external system that no other source records: a decision, an approval, an input, an external operation or an acceptance, with its question, options and consequences, recommendation, recipient (principal, role or `unassigned`), qualified targets (`requirement:R4` inside the origin spec, or `xref:…`), per-phase effects (`--effect closeout:block` lets implementation continue) and an optional subject (`--subject requirement:R4` or `path:<file>`) whose change invalidates an answer recorded for the old content. Open one only when a different answer would materially change execution, scope, authority, risk acceptance, closeout or publication and no authorization already given covers it. Each request is an append-only journal under `.pose/actions/`, read whole: a line with a second value, a duplicate field (keys differing only in case included), an unknown event type, another schema, a sequence gap, an empty line or no terminator is never part of a view; `action show|list` and MCP locate it (line, byte offset, length, SHA-256) as an `interrupted-tail`, with a truncation proposal for a person to review, or as `corrupt`, to restore from version control — the engine repairs nothing (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`); `open` is a preview until `--apply`. Targets are qualified with the current project id; when it is derived from the directory name (no `.pose/project.json`, `POSE_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID` or `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding), `open --apply` says so (on stderr with `--json`), because a checkout under another name would record another project, and the obligation snapshot lists the same limitation. A resolution names the `--request-digest` it answers and the `--expected-revision` it read, carries an `--idempotency-key` (a retry is a no-op, the same key with other content is refused, a second answer against the same revision is refused) and comes from an actor holding the recipient role in `.pose/policy/actions.json` (`{\"schema_version\":1,\"roles\":{\"maintainer\":[\"human:<name>\"]},\"identity_assurance\":\"declared|verified\"}`); under `verified` it needs `--claim <file>` with an Ed25519 claim from an issuer pinned in the review policy, bound to project, audience, request digest, principal and answer (a human principal also needs `human_authority_issuers`). Answered is not satisfied: a declined approval, a rejected acceptance or a failed external operation leaves the condition unmet. The requester may cancel its own question; a waiver needs the recipient authority and a reason. Open requests are projected as `actor-action` obligations in `state --attention` and `pose_obligations`; MCP opens requests with `pose_action_open` (risk class `governance-write`, a preview unless `apply` is true) and reads them with `pose_action_requests`; `pose_action_resolve` (spec `pose-mcp-action-resolve-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an answer only with the principal's proof — a signature by a key registered to it, or a trusted issuer's claim — and refuses a declared answer under any assurance; its preview returns the statement to sign and the `ssh-keygen` command.\nPOSE.md:397:- Phase readiness (spec `pose-phase-scoped-readiness`) — `pose_spec_readiness` with `phases: true`, and `state --attention --scope spec:<slug>`, report per phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`) whether it is `clear`, `restricted`, `partially-restricted` on named nodes or `unknown`. A phase is `clear` only when no obligation restricts it and every producer that could restrict it was read; a restriction on `R4` alone is reported as partial with the note that independence of the rest is not demonstrated. `ready` keeps its legacy meaning: partial eligibility never turns it true.\nPOSE.md:426:- `amend` — append-only spec amendment history (`amendments.jsonl` beside a folder spec's `spec.md`, or `YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.amendments.jsonl` beside a flat dated spec; the spec is resolved through the store, so both layouts amend alike). `--baseline` snapshots every R-ID hash; `--ids R2 --change added|withdrawn|semantic|editorial --rationale <text> --author @alias [--reviewer @alias]` acknowledges a material change; `--list` renders history and pending acknowledgments. On `done` specs with a history, `lint-spec` rejects any requirement whose current text is not acknowledged by an event — specs cannot be silently rewritten after evidence. `--nodes [--json]` prints the versioned contract-node projection: requirements, assumptions and decisions with namespace, content hash, state and relations. With `contract_nodes_version: 1` adopted in the review policy, `--baseline` writes a schema-2 R/A/D snapshot, `--ids` accepts A/D, `--change transition` acknowledges an allowed state change, `editorial` is refused when a node changed state or relations, and the gate also rejects an active decision resting on an invalidated or withdrawn assumption. Without the capability the gate stays requirement-only and a schema-2 event is refused; schema-1 logs keep their meaning.\nPOSE.md:444:### MCP connection contract\nPOSE.md:471:## 7) CI policy\nPOSE.md:482:### Interpreting failures\nPOSE.md:488:### Phased rollout (recommended)\nPOSE.md:495:### Validation matrix per stack/module\nPOSE.md:506:## 8) `.pose/knowledge/` governance\nPOSE.md:532:## 9) Instance limitations\nPOSE.md:543:## 10) Instance next steps\nPOSE.md:555:## 11) POSE engine limitations & community feedback\nPOSE.md:568:## 12) Executive summary\nPOSE.md:577:## Open-Source POSE Contributor Mode\nPOSE.md:590:## Opt-in progressive review profiles (ABM)\nPOSE.md:610:### Bands and the declared forecast\nPOSE.md:634:### Observed structure and causal mapping\nPOSE.md:729:### Protected policy baseline\nPOSE.md:757:## Verified review authority (ABM)\nPOSE.md:796:### Historical review claims and criterion reuse\nPOSE.md:819:### Action lifecycle authority\nPOSE.md:831:### Action recovery and preflight\nPOSE.md:841:### Amendment history before close\nPOSE.md:847:### Terminal closeout verification\nPOSE.md:853:### Adopt delegated review\nPOSE.md:901:### Delegated executions (`pose delegate`)\nPOSE.md:933:#### Retiring a personal launcher\nPOSE.md:944:### Delegated review attempts and history\nPOSE.md:960:### Keep delegated work proportional\nPOSE.md:999:### Bound delegated adapter execution\nPOSE.md:1029:### Continue interrupted delegated reviews\nPOSE.md:1049:### Verify signed delegated run provenance\nPOSE.md:1078:**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit; `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:9:// of them and say what it swept (R1–R5, D2). The draft is the fenced\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:56:\t// pose-delegated-review-conclusions R1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:60:\t// Limit names the declared accepted limit the finding falls inside\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:61:\t// (spec pose-review-declared-threat-model R2).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:66:// a recorded attestation does (spec pose-delegated-review-conclusions R1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:87:// ReviewDraftPrior is a draft's account of one prior finding (R3, R4).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:102:\t// attestation (spec pose-delegated-review-conclusions R1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:107:\t// pose-delegated-verdict-destinations R1, R2).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:151:// Prior finding statuses (R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:164:// read as absent (review round 2, R2-B2). Every decision line outside the\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:165:// block must agree with it (R2-B1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:176:\t\t// one, and any other is an error (review round 3, R3-B1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:355:// was carried, the account the last round gave it (R2, R5).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:387:// last, the run id breaking a tie (review round 2, R2-F1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:405:// scope and the run history (R1). A history or a bundle inventory with a loss\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:530:// ValidateRoundDraft judges a draft of round against R3 and R4: from round 2\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:545:\t\t// would count as convergence without a defect (review round 2, R2-B3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:585:// from the ledger, byte-stable for the same ledger state (R2, R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:655:// threshold; each opens a new session, recording why (R6, D1, D5).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:708:// pose-delegated-review-expired-session R1, D1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:738:// ReviewRoundMetric is the convergence of one scope's rounds (R7, R10).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:751:// (R10). Shares are of the prior findings accounted for in valid drafts.\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:755:\t// approved, if one did (R7 per kind, R10; review round 2, R2-B4).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_rounds.go:804:\t\t// decides (review round 3, R3-F1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go:385:\t// Limit is the declared accepted limit a finding disposed within-limit\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go:386:\t// falls inside (spec pose-review-declared-threat-model R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go:475:\t// pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit R4).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go:933:\t\t// opted into component-aware planning remain readable (spec R29).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go:2466:// pose-review-approval-follows-children R2, R5).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go:2704:\t\t// pose-delegated-verdict-destinations R1; review round 1, B05).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:70:\t// (spec pose-review-readiness-before-seal R4).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:80:\t// (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:83:\t// accepted limits the bundle sealed and how a finding inside one is\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:84:\t// classified (spec pose-review-declared-threat-model R2).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:146:\treturn text + \" (j) the `evidence` of a `passed` criterion is of a class the criterion asks for and, when the criterion or tool belongs to a component, of that component (the Sealed evidence list names the module of each ref); (k) the `basis` of a mapping is a requirement or decision id of the scope's spec, such as R1 or D1.\"\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:193:// pose-review-conclude-ignores-unrelated-local-edits R1).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:470:\t\t\tline(\"The scope's spec declares no accepted limit: every finding is judged on its own.\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:582:\t\treturn \", `prior_findings` (each with `id`, `status` and `evidence`, one for every finding of the previous round; the `id` is the identifier listed under Findings to account for, which carries its round, for example R2-F1, never a bare F1, and a finding you report now takes an `id` that no earlier round used, for example N1: a bare or reused identifier makes the whole draft unreadable) and `sweep` (what you swept)\"\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
