{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n \"pose-review-declared-threat-model|accepted limit|accepted_limits|threat model\" .pose/knowledge .pose/adr .pose/specs 2>/dev/null | head -n 150'","aggregated_output":".pose/adr/2026-07-19-slsa-build-l2-provenance-claim.md:14:Builder threat model: GitHub-hosted runners execute the release workflow.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:2:slug: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:16:# Spec: pose-review-declared-threat-model\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:24:Each of the 23 rounds of `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks` found a more remote adversarial variant: concurrent HEAD movement, case folding, Unicode normalization, symlinks inside submodules. Every one was real, but no round could converge, because the spec had no place to say which environment the guarantee assumes. A cap on rounds would approve something unreviewed; a declared threat model converges by explicit classification.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:38:- R4: `lint-spec --ready-check` shall require accepted limits to be justified and the ADR, when present, to reference them.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:55:- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:56:- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits.go\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:57:- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/accepted_limits_test.go\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:69:- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/accepted_limits_lint_test.go\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:86:A spec section `### Accepted limits` with `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` entries. The review plan and the bundle seal them as `accepted_limits` (omitted when none, so every other plan and bundle keeps its digest). A draft finding may carry `limit`; the attestation records it with disposition `within-limit`. Brief template `review-brief/11`. `lint-spec --ready-check` checks the section.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:90:`accepted_limits` in review plans and bundles and `limit` in draft and attestation findings, all omitted when empty.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:129:- 2026-11-25: implemented R1–R4: `ParseAcceptedLimits`, the plan and bundle `accepted_limits`, the draft `limit` and the `within-limit` disposition, `review-brief/11` and the ready check. Each has a test that fails with it mutated out; the first digest test survived because the spec edit changed the scope digest too, and now compares the plan's digest with and without the limits.\n.pose/specs/2026-11-23-pose-review-declared-threat-model.md:154:Specs declare accepted limits; the review plan and bundle seal them; `review-brief/11` lists them and tells the reviewer to classify a finding inside one with `limit`; the attestation records it `within-limit`, which does not block an approval; `lint-spec --ready-check` requires each limit to say why it is accepted and an ADR citing the spec to name it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
